our day not  reactive don't answer any emails etc  emails are not the issue here the issue  here is being shouted at by a kid who  doesn't want to do her teeth or wants to  watch more videos while she does her  teeth but then she doesn't pay attention  and she doesn't brush her teeth properly  i feel like i've done 10 rounds with  mike tyson before i've even started my  day and i'm not really in a proactive  headspace in fact i'm barely even  functioning  so morning routines are off the table  and so too are just routines in general  because any time you think you've got  into this like nice routine flow  something will come along and like blast  out the water whether that's one of the  kids getting covered for example which  means that they can't go to preschool  whether it's one of us getting poorly  and we get poorly all the time because  the kid brings home all these diseases  from preschool which makes us sick also  all tired and under asleep also my son  will actually wipe his butt and then  wipe his hand across your face in just  this big smear or maybe you need to  paint the house and normally that  wouldn't be an issue but you can't paint  the house with the kids in the house  they need to be shipped off which means  you all need to go and stay with your  in-laws and then the painted decorators  don't show up and perhaps this was an  honest mistake so then you arrange it  for another time but then the in-laws  get covered so it has to be rearranged  again and each time you have to  completely change your schedule your  your content schedule you have to  rethink about when you're going to film  it when you're going to edit it where  are you going to be when you're training  this is just a hypothetical example you  understand point is you don't get any  routine but also you are stuck rigidly  to this routine i can't just jet off to  another country and film a cool video  there even though i'd love to and if i  injure myself i can't just take some  time off to recover properly because i'm  a parent and i have to stick to this  routine and that involves being jumped  on all the time  goals are completely different and  there's this unspoken assumption in a  lot of content that you're kind of  training to attract members of the  opposite sex and particularly with  productivity and self-improvement you're  trying to attract the opposite sex  there's a whole kind of subculture of  that on youtube and as a parent as  someone who's happily married that's  obviously not my goal or my interest  anymore some information on how to build  the perfect v taper because that's what  feels like a t-shirt and looks great is  not particularly interesting to me and  the same goes i imagine for a lot of  other parents as well we just want to be  healthy and feel good about ourselves  and our goals change in terms of  productivity as well so these days i  realize i want to spend more time with  my family and  success for the sake of success isn't  really worth it i could grow this  channel a lot faster if i invested in it  more if i hired lots of people teams of  marketers etc but in doing that i then  become this kind of ceo guy i'd have a  lot more stress that i'd be responsible  for them as well as for my children and  my family i don't have a job where yeah  i'm successful and yeah i'm doing things  i enjoy but it's not taking loads of  time away from my family life or  committing me to things that i don't  necessarily want to be committed to i  don't want to be successful at the  expense of being a good dad and i think  a lot of mums and dads watching this  might hopefully feel the same way too  your priorities shift and a lot of the  youtube content out there just doesn't  reflect this  so then all this raises the question  should you even become a parent at a  young age if you're interested in  getting to your best ever shape if you  want to build a business or thrive in  some kind of other career path someone  who had an interesting perspective on  this is captain sinbad i'm a fan of his  channel i love the production value and  the way he draws on movies for  inspiration in a similar way that i do  and i feel like he's actually trying to  make art here not just youtube talking  head we're actually at a similar level  in terms of our growth he doesn't know  this but i'm secretly competing with him  because i'm a maniac but in one of his  videos he referenced the book and to  paraphrase it was basically saying that  becoming a parent at young age is one of  the worst things you can do if you want  to be successful because it takes up so  many resources so much time so much  energy and you just can't build an  empire when you're also trying to be a  parent and this is something that i  disagree with i would not advise any  single people out there to avoid  starting a family if they want to be  successful in fact quite the opposite  personally i'm actually financially far  more stable now than i ever was when i  was single and part of the reason for  this is being a parent which lights a  fire under your ass and make sure that  you really focus on what you're doing  when emmy came along that's when i  decided to launch an ebook and sell it  from the site which ultimately was a big  success more so than i expected and it's  what led to me being able to do the  bioneer as my full-time job i also did  this in a very careful way i still work  for android authority i was still a  freelance writer and i kept those things  going because i didn't want to take a  leap of faith and potentially jeopardize  the you know financial future of my  family at the same time being a parent  just grows you up and i don't want to be  one of those people who condescends to  non-parents like you can't possibly know  what it's like but it does really mature  you in some fundamental ways that are  hard to explain clearly i'm still a  man-child but at the same time i also am  much more restrained and i'd like to  think i'm more patient and more  understanding of other people a little  bit more measured in my opinions and  i've had experience being a genuine  leader which is something that anyone  can experience by being a parent it's  changed the way i approach things and  honestly i think i'm more successful  because of that growth and maturity that  i've achieved you know the first time  you get a job you grow up in a lot of  ways and you can undeniably say you've  changed between getting that job and not  having the job the same thing happens  when you become a parent you change in a  fundamental way and on the whole i think  it's for the better it forces you to  grow up in those key areas  by the way my wife always said that she  wasn't attracted to muscular men when we  first started dating so it didn't even  help me in that regard she's also very  unimpressed with like traditional  markers of success and i love her so  much for that the great thing about  being in a happy family is it takes all  this external pressure off you know i'm  not trying to impress anybody i'm happy  i don't need external validation in the  same way because i get it from my family  so it just frees you it's very  liberating and of course i should say  that having children is like absolutely  amazing they're hilarious they're  fascinating they're extremely cute and  yeah they become your best little  buddies  and the other thing is being a parent  doesn't stop you from achieving the  things that you want to achieve like  genuinely so many guys i know have ended  promising relationships because they  felt they're being held back by their  partners they want to travel the world  or start a business focus on their  career get into amazing shape  but in every single case where that's  happened that person they'd then not go  on to travel the world or to start an  amazing business they just kind of  floundered and eventually ended up in a  different relationship on the other hand  i know plenty of people who've been  highly successful and they're in  relationships in a lot of cases here the  other half and the children are useful  scapegoats for why your life isn't the  way you wanted to be i've always been of  the philosophy that if there's two  things i want and i'm forced to choose  between them my answer is always do both  and there's no reason you can't be  productive successful train hard and  raise a family and we'll get to some  tips on how to do that in a moment and  in terms of getting strong and into  shape with kids actually in some ways  you get into even better shape because  having kids means you're constantly  active constantly moving i'm being  climbed on all the time by my kids we  play a game called playground where i  have to do kettlebell swings with my  daughter she calls that the swing i have  to do leg raises for hanging onto my  legs that's the seesaw sometimes i'm  going up and down the stairs carrying  both children one on each arm my  daughter's 17 kilograms i think my son  is about eight kilograms that's a lot of  weight to be carrying all the time you  guys know how much i agree with you know  constantly training throughout the day  and in this way dad strength is very  real and very similar to farmer strength  so then how do we work around the  limitations the challenges of having  kids if you want to be productive and  get into amazing shape well my first  piece of advice in terms of getting into  shape and training is to make your  workouts more adaptable so that you can  train under a range of different  circumstances one way i've done this is  that i've written multiple versions of  the same workout so i have three  workouts that i alternate between at the  moment they're kind of based on the  protein performance system super  functional training 2.0 but i've adapted  them to my current needs and then i do a  version of those depending on where i am  so one version is suitable for the gym  which is great if i want the weights  access to more tools there's a version i  do when i'm outside if i'm going to use  my kettlebell and my  medicine ball and run around the field  somewhere then there's a version that i  do if i'm in the office because i've got  some equipment here that i train with  and there's a version i can do at home  whilst my wife is going through the  shower in the evening before bed  obviously i don't have a bench press at  home but by doing high repetition  push-ups or by doing dips i can somewhat  train in an equivalent manner and this  actually just keeps things fresh and  interesting but it also means that if i  can't get to the gym that doesn't mean  that i don't train another thing i do is  that i have essential exercises the  things i absolutely must do then i have  bonus additional peripheral exercises so  i might go to the gym and if i've only  got 25 minutes in the gym before i need  to start work then i'll do those  essential things there and that's at  least something at the same time the  peripheral exercises the one i leave at  the end they tend to be the ones that i  can do somewhere else so i might do my  big bulky workout in the morning at the  gym and then in the evening i'll finish  up with some mobility work with maybe  some handstand practice or maybe some  light push-ups and things this way i'm  spreading my workouts for the day which  you guys know is something that i highly  advocate but also i'm adapting my  training to my situation i would also  say that with regards to the routine  changing without warning you should also  always train when the opportunity arises  so don't take too many rest days if you  don't need them because you might need  them later when the boiler breaks or  something then of course i also  recommend training throughout the day  wherever possible breaking your workouts  into smaller workouts is something i've  talked about a lot on this channel one  of the reasons i do this is because i'm  a parent and it's a great way to fit it  in so i'll do a 10 minute workout here  10 minute workout there everyone has 10  minutes in the day and i made a whole  video discussing how 10 minutes in a day  can be enough to train another thing to  think about is just efficiency as in how  quickly can you get into the gym do a  workout that's going to stimulate to  growth and adaptation and get out this  is something that will come with time as  you get more practiced you'll learn  specifically what your body responds to  and how to feel when you've done enough  and get that minimum effective dose  for me the biggest issue with training  as a parent isn't that i don't have time  and it's not even that i don't have  energy i think that everyone has at  least a little bit of time if you've  watched anything on tv in the last week  you had a little bit of time the issue  is sometimes energy but the other issue  and the big one for me is that there's  so much i want to do and there's not  enough time to do all those things so  it's not that i don't want to work out  because i really enjoy working out but  it's just that there might be something  that's even more tempting like at the  moment i'm playing shenmue 3 as i've  randomly talked about a weird amount on  this channel i also install some  emulators and i'm playing the old virtue  of fighter games i really want to watch  doctor strange now it's on streaming  wife and i still haven't watched top gun  there are books i want to read comics  i've downloaded the latest iron man  comic and the latest dragon ball super  manga haven't even had a chance to read  them when it comes to the evening and  i've got 10 minutes to fit in this  workout sometimes the allure of virtua  fighter or dragon ball super is a little  bit too strong and i'd rather do that  one solution to this is multitasking  your workouts so that could mean that  you're watching tv whilst you're  training or it might just mean that  between sets you did a little bit of  reading or between sets you do a little  bit of gaming and one of the reasons i  wanted to talk about this is because so  many sites and so many youtube channels  talk about this like it's the devil like  you must focus 100 on your workouts at  all times and that's great if you have  loads of time but my ethos on this is  that training in a distracted way is  better than not training at all  as for the sleep this is something  that's a little bit harder to negotiate  some people tend to take power naps but  again i think this is advice that isn't  always that in touch with reality  personally i and my wife find that power  naps make us feel really groggy when we  wake up and if the issue is time because  they have so many chores to do because  that's the thing with kids you don't  just do they're looking after the kids  you have to make all those weird purees  for them and you have to clean up the 10  times as much crockery because you know  you have more mouths to feed you have to  cook for more people you have to clean  all the time and they leave their junk  everywhere so you don't really have time  to power nap if you pound nap then the  house is going to be a tip or you're not  going to do that training all i'll say  is that while i don't want to downplay  the importance of sleep  you don't have to fret that much if you  only get six hours or even five hours a  few nights because this is a rite of  passage that every parent goes through  and it doesn't kill them the book why we  sleep by matthew walker actually makes  quite a lot of sweeping statements about  how a lack of sleep can double your  chances of cancer and all sorts of  things like that how it massively hurts  productivity increases injuries and  accidents and while there might be some  truth in this actually also found a  really interesting article online that  countered many of these points with  scientific literature saying that it's  not as clear-cut as we might think and  that's kind of like my gut impulse as  well because if that were true then  parents wouldn't live as long as  non-parents and actually we know that  the opposite is true parents live longer  than non-parents one of the reasons for  this might be because they pick up more  infections from preschool which actually  strengthens the immune system and for me  the worst thing i can do if i'm tired is  to fret about it make it into this big  issue especially if i hit the snooze  button if i lay around on the couch if i  tell people i'm tired conversely if i  just get to work smile put on some  upbeat music pretty quickly i forget  that i'm that tired and at the same time  if i'm moving throughout the days i've  talked about this is a great way to  stimulate yourself and provide yourself  with more energy as arnold  schwarzenegger himself would say sleep  quicker  in terms of productivity and achieving  the things you want to in business and  your career i will just say that once  again many of the same things apply you  need to focus on the essentials and then  just do the extra bit at the end of the  day if you have time that means for me  every week i have to make a video and  that's 100 the priority i don't even  really answer emails until that point  which means i do get some angry emails  later in the week that i have to deal  with but if i were to sit and answer  them and to write to-do lists and have  meetings with people who you know are  just gonna blow smoke then i wouldn't  really be being productive and i won't  be using my time maximally you want to  create a proper separation between work  and home but again being adaptable is  useful in this regard the right  technology can really help for instance  i just got a new laptop for editing on  the go it's made all the difference to  me we'll also say that you can multitask  again and in particular that means  planning whilst you're doing other stuff  so when i'm cooking when i'm on the  toilet i'll often be planning in my head  the next script the next video thinking  of all the different shots and i'll do  this for ages or listen to podcasts for  research or youtube videos for research  and i'll bring it all together so that  when it's time to film on monday i can  go straight into it and i know exactly  what i want to film  to step back and look at the bigger  picture you also need to be really clear  what your goals are and what you want  because think not only about the job in  terms of you know the glossy front of it  what you think it's going to be like but  also the day-to-day sure you might like  to be a powerful ceo who has lots of  influence and cool suits and a high-rise  building but then think about what  that's actually like what hours are  going to end up working is that  something you're happy to do as a parent  what's the commute like how stressful  would you be coming home i'd just like  to take a moment to make it really clear  that it's absolutely fine to not have  any big ambitions in terms of your  career etc there's this contingent on  the web that implies that if you're not  interested in becoming a movie star or a  business person or an entrepreneur then  you know you're wasting your time you're  never going to amount to anything and  your life doesn't matter etc i don't  think it's going to be further from the  truth there's absolutely nothing more  noble about being a movie star you know  an entrepreneur than there is if you  just want to have a nice life for your  family that's a perfectly fine  aspiration likewise it's perfectly fine  if you do want to change the world with  some kind of great ambition or idea just  keep in mind that you're not obligated  to be highly ambitious or to try and  change the world or do any of these  things and it's absolutely fine if  you're just happy ticking along and you  want to get your sense of meaning and  fulfillment from elsewhere finally i'd  say make sure you have your priorities  straight and that means not letting your  work bleed into your time with your kids  ultimately my big takeaway from all this  is that you can achieve all the things  you want to achieve in terms of your  fitness and your career and everything  else and you shouldn't view your family  as this kind of like big challenge to  overcome you just might need to adapt  some of the advice you've heard online  because so much of that is aimed  squarely at single 20-somethings very  few responsibilities and for a large  proportion of we youtube viewing public  that's simply not the reality hope you  found some useful tips in this video to  take away like i said i'd love to hear  your tips down below there's a lack of  advice for parents something that you've  noticed on youtube well there's some big  youtube channels that i'm missing this  video is a bit of a risk because i don't  know how many of you guys have kids that  would be interested in this topic and  i've ranted for ages but they're just  some points i really wanted to share and  hopefully some of you guys found it  interesting i'll be interested to see  how this video performs subscribe if you  want to see more like this and also not  that much like this and guys remember  that link in the description if you want  a seven day free trial of blinkist hit  the bell button for notifications and  either way thank you so much for  watching this one guys and i'll see you  next time  bye for now
 

